  "id": "Compound::DB01050 - Gene::9356",
  "verbalization": "Ibuprofen is implicated in the pathogenesis of several inflammatory conditions, including necrotising fasciitis, cellulitis gangrenous, and renal papillary necrosis, all of which are associated with Sulindac or Etodolac. These compounds bind to Slc22a6, a transporter protein involved in the uptake of monocarboxylic acids, suggesting a potential mechanism by which Ibuprofen may influence Slc22a6 function. The shared lowest common ancestors—Monocarboxylic Acid and Enzyme Inhibitor—highlight a common biological pathway, possibly involving the inhibition of metabolic processes or enzymatic activities that are regulated by Slc22a6. The evidence suggests that Ibuprofen may indirectly affect Slc22a6 through its interaction with these upstream compounds, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated. The consistent association across different disease pathways supports a role for Slc22a6 in the pharmacological effects of Ibuprofen, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the direct interaction.",
